1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,161
To sum from m to n, subtract the sum up to m minus one from the sum up to n. Subtract up to m itself and you throw away a term you were meant to keep.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,444
Checking a formula for n equal to one, two and three proves nothing about n equal to forty.

3
00:00:15,444 --> 00:00:19,246
Induction proves it for every natural number at once.

4
00:00:19,246 --> 00:00:21,367
Think of an endless row of dominoes.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,425
Two things make them all fall.

6
00:00:23,425 --> 00:00:28,320
First, the basis: push the first one, so P of one is true.

7
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:35,482
Second, the inductive step: the dominoes are close enough that whenever one falls, it knocks over the next.

8
00:00:35,482 --> 00:00:39,843
P of k true forces P of k plus one true.

9
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:40,901
Watch.

10
00:00:40,901 --> 00:00:45,445
Push the first, and each one topples the next, all the way along.

11
00:00:45,445 --> 00:00:48,242
Leave out either step and the row stays standing.

12
00:00:49,466 --> 00:00:54,142
Examiners give marks for the layout itself, so learn these four lines.

13
00:00:54,142 --> 00:00:55,418
Name the statement.

14
00:00:55,418 --> 00:00:56,759
Prove the basis.

15
00:00:56,759 --> 00:01:00,900
Assume it for k, and say the words inductive hypothesis.

16
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:04,373
Then prove it for k plus one, and write the conclusion.

17
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:07,592
The cube formula, by induction.

18
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:09,619
Basis: both sides are one.

19
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:12,178
Assume it is true for k.

20
00:01:12,178 --> 00:01:13,947
That is the inductive hypothesis.

21
00:01:14,166 --> 00:01:22,006
Add the next term, k plus one cubed, to both sides, and replace the first k terms by the assumed formula.

22
00:01:22,006 --> 00:01:28,811
Take out k plus one squared over four: the bracket is k squared plus four k plus four.

23
00:01:29,033 --> 00:01:31,665
That is k plus two, squared.

24
00:01:31,665 --> 00:01:35,844
Exactly the formula with k plus one in place of n.

25
00:01:35,844 --> 00:01:38,186
So by induction it holds for all n.

26
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:42,725
Induction also proves divisibility.

27
00:01:42,933 --> 00:01:45,798
Basis: three squared minus one is eight.

28
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,616
Assume three to the two k, minus one, is eight m for some whole number m.

29
00:01:52,616 --> 00:01:55,903
So three to the two k is eight m plus one.

30
00:01:56,133 --> 00:02:06,838
Then three to the two k plus two, minus one, is nine times that, minus one: seventy two m plus eight, which is eight times nine m plus one.

31
00:02:06,838 --> 00:02:07,917
Divisible by eight.

32
00:02:09,933 --> 00:02:11,063
One with fractions.

33
00:02:11,266 --> 00:02:13,592
Basis: a half equals a half.

34
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:19,492
Assume the sum to k terms is k over k plus one, and add the next term.

35
00:02:19,492 --> 00:02:26,780
Over a common denominator the top is k squared plus two k plus one: k plus one, squared.

36
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,463
Cancel, and you get k plus one over k plus two: the formula for k plus one.

37
00:02:35,466 --> 00:02:41,548
The classic mistake: rewriting the right hand side with k plus one and calling it done.

38
00:02:41,548 --> 00:02:42,942
That proves nothing.

39
00:02:42,942 --> 00:02:45,051
You must use the hypothesis.

40
00:02:45,051 --> 00:02:48,619
And a proof without the basis step loses half the marks.

41
00:02:51,033 --> 00:02:52,246
Pause and think.

42
00:02:52,246 --> 00:02:53,427
Which step comes first?

43
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:02,528
P of one, the basis step.

44
00:03:02,528 --> 00:03:05,890
Without it there is no first domino, and nothing ever falls.

45
00:03:10,233 --> 00:03:15,117
Unit two built the exponential series from the limit for e.

46
00:03:15,117 --> 00:03:16,341
Here it is at work.

47
00:03:16,341 --> 00:03:19,102
The white curve is e to the x.

48
00:03:19,102 --> 00:03:25,222
One term of the series is a flat line; two terms a straight line; three a parabola.

49
00:03:25,222 --> 00:03:32,447
Each new term bends the yellow curve closer, and by six or seven terms it hugs e to the x across the whole picture.

50
00:03:33,666 --> 00:03:41,324
The logarithmic series behaves differently: it only works for x between minus one and one, including one.

51
00:03:41,324 --> 00:03:48,668
At x equal to one the partial sums zig zag, above and below, closing in on log two.

52
00:03:48,668 --> 00:03:55,386
At x equal to minus one point two the terms grow and the sums run away: no sum at all.

53
00:03:55,386 --> 00:03:58,234
That is why the condition must always be stated.

54
00:03:59,466 --> 00:04:00,742
Recognise the series.

55
00:04:00,966 --> 00:04:05,458
The first is the series for e with its leading one removed: e minus one.

56
00:04:05,666 --> 00:04:09,743
For the second, write e and e to the minus one.

57
00:04:09,743 --> 00:04:13,525
Subtracting kills the even terms and doubles the odd ones.

58
00:04:13,733 --> 00:04:21,302
So the sum is e minus e to the minus one, over two: one point one seven five two.

59
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:24,851
A fast-converging series.

60
00:04:25,066 --> 00:04:31,324
Choose x so that one plus x over one minus x is one point two: x is one eleventh.

61
00:04:31,533 --> 00:04:33,591
Small x, so the terms shrink fast.

62
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:36,654
Three terms give point one eight two three.

63
00:04:38,666 --> 00:04:46,890
And remember: here log means the natural logarithm, base e. Read it as base ten and every answer is off by a factor.

64
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:50,539
Pause and think.

65
00:04:50,539 --> 00:04:52,309
The sum of the first ten cubes.

66
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:03,082
Three thousand and twenty five: the square of fifty five.

67
00:05:03,082 --> 00:05:08,669
Three eighty five is the sum of the squares, and five thousand and fifty belongs to Gauss.

68
00:05:10,666 --> 00:05:11,566
One more.

69
00:05:11,566 --> 00:05:12,803
Careful with the first term.

70
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:20,691
e plus one.

71
00:05:20,691 --> 00:05:27,319
The series for e already starts one plus one over one factorial; this one has an extra one in front.

72
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:30,582
Quick recap.

73
00:05:30,582 --> 00:05:35,504
The three standard sums, with denominators two, six and four.

74
00:05:35,504 --> 00:05:40,647
Any polynomial term: split, substitute, factorise, test.

75
00:05:40,647 --> 00:05:44,436
For a part sum, subtract up to m minus one.

76
00:05:44,436 --> 00:05:48,955
Induction needs a basis and a step that uses the hypothesis.

77
00:05:48,955 --> 00:05:56,779
And the exponential series works for every x, while the log series needs x between minus one and one.

78
00:06:00,966 --> 00:06:03,038
That was sequence and series.

79
00:06:03,038 --> 00:06:09,652
Next, matrices: solving systems of linear equations with determinants and inverses.

80
00:06:09,652 --> 00:06:10,248
See you there.

